      "category": "culture",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "The Erickson Log House Museum showcases a 1868 log home built by Johannes Erickson and his 13‑year‑old son Alfred.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        22
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Scandia%2C_Minnesota",
      "category": "landmark",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "Scandia is the site of Minnesota’s first Swedish immigrant settlement, with the first log cabin erected on Hay Lake in 1850.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        14,
        15
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Scandia%2C_Minnesota",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "The Washington County Barn Quilt Trail winds 16.4 miles through Scandia, featuring 18 quilts inspired by “The Quiltmaker’s Gift.”.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        24,
        26
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Scandia%2C_Minnesota",
      "category": "landmark",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "Scandia’s historic village area centers around the intersection of Oakhill Road, a reminder of its early township layout.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        9
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Scandia%2C_Minnesota",
      "category": "landmark",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 6,
      "text": "The city’s name, Scandia, reflects its Scandinavian roots, echoing the heritage of its first settlers.",
